{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310850,"nonce":"7JH8KBhtuBW72VhZCW7pqWo+uNw=","title":"MoveFlow SHIPPED: a SymbolId-keyed forward may-analysis over the MIR CFG replaces the TAST move checker for @:move, plus @:borrow/@:owned ...","message":"**SHIPPED 2026-08-20**, 71c6554e + 0527f7f8 + 383b735e + 1ce87835 + da9cc5a8. `compiler/src/ir/mir/moveflow.rs`.\nOracle `compiler/tests/move/check.sh` **33/33** (was 3/5, and 2 of those 3\npassed vacuously). 184/184. Cold compile 3.15s vs 3.20s baseline — no cost.\n**The nue bundle is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the pre-MoveFlow baseline** (`cmp` on the\n.rzb) — that is the proof the analysis emits no opcodes, and it is the check to\nrepeat rather than arguing from tok/s, which the box will not resolve.\n\n\n- **Keyed on SymbolId, never IrId.** `var d = b` needs no cast, so `d` and `b`\n  ARE the same register. A register-keyed check cannot tell their reads apart —\n  that is what made the old one trap correct code (fixed f0e373b7).\n- **Recorder during lowering**, checker at the tail. Events `Bind | Move | Read`\n  carry (block, order, symbol, TAST SourceLocation). Needs no `byte_offset` on\n  `IrSourceLocation` and no `register_to_symbol` map.\n- **NEVER read the instruction stream.** `IrInstruction::uses()` returns `[src]`\n  for BOTH `MarkMoved` and `CheckLive`, so a MIR use/def scan counts a move as a\n  use of itself at its own program point — re-importing the same-location\n  cancellation that makes the TAST checker blind.\n- **Move is CHECKED THEN SET.** That, and only that, is what makes `f(a); g(a)`\n  fire. Nothing anywhere compares two source locations.\n- **Bind KILLS** (reassignment revives). **Fixpoint first, report second**, or a\n  loop body reports once per iteration.\n- Witness join = **min by order, and the Move transfer takes min too** — \"keep\n  whatever arrived\" is NON-MONOTONE and can oscillate forever.\n- Unreachable blocks keep bottom and report nothing. That is what makes\n  `if (f) return take(r); return r.v;` silent.\n\n\n| site | file |\n| --- | --- |\n| `var x = …` Bind, RHS-variable Move | `stmt/statements.rs` Let |\n| assignment Bind + RHS Move | `stmt/statements.rs` Assign |\n| every variable Read | `expr/variables.rs` |\n| call/ctor **argument** Move | `expr/dispatch.rs::record_argument_moves` |\n| closure **capture** Read | `expr/dispatch.rs::record_capture_reads` |\n| by-value `@:move` params | `expr/functions.rs` → `enroll_move_params` |\n\n`check_move_flow()` is hooked in front of **every** `builder.finish_function()`\nunder `ir/mir` (23 sites) and `reset_move_recorder()` beside every\n`strict_move_locals.clear()`. Events must never cross a function boundary —\nreplayed against another CFG they invent control flow.\n\n**TRAP, cost me a false positive:** a method call is desugared in tast_to_hir to\n`method(receiver, args…)` with `is_method: true`, so THE RECEIVER IS args[0].\nNot skipping it makes `a.get(); a.get()` a double move. A receiver is a borrow\n(no `@:borrow` on params yet), so skip `usize::from(is_method)`.\n\n**Argument moves are recorded AFTER the call lowers**, never before: the arg's\nown Read is recorded while it lowers, and a Move ordered ahead of it makes the\nFIRST call report against itself.\n\n\n`derive_move_classes` is per-context, so an imported `@:move` class was\ncompletely unchecked. Fixed with `MOVE_CLASS_NAMES` (canonical-name keyed,\nsharing `CLASS_NAME_ALIAS` poisoning) beside `CLASS_FIELD_LAYOUTS` in\n`ir/mir/mod.rs`; consult it through `type_is_move_class(ty)`, never\n`derive_move_classes.contains` directly. Probe: `RAYZOR_DEBUG_MOVECLASS=1`\nprints every class a context lowers plus each record/lookup.\n\n**TRAP:** in a LOOSE directory (no rayzor.toml) an imported user class is never\nlowered to MIR in the importing compilation AT ALL — its `@:move` is invisible\nand its fields give E0100. That is the pre-existing cross-module declaration\ngap, not a move-checker gap. Oracle case `c6_cross_file` therefore carries its\nown `rayzor.toml`; a flat two-file repro proves nothing.\n\nResidual: the registry is process-global and order-dependent — an importer\nlowered BEFORE its import would miss the annotation (silent, not unsound).\n\n\nMIR diagnostics were PRINTED and then ignored — compilation continued and the\ncache was populated, so the SECOND run skipped lowering and reported nothing.\n`compile_ast_with_shared_state` now returns Err on Error-severity E0382/E0300\n(one summary line; the spans already rendered). `check.sh` clears `.rayzor`\nbefore every case for the same reason.\n\n\nThe syntax ALREADY PARSED: `FunctionParam.meta` exists and\n`@:borrow r:Res` compiled long before this. The annotation was simply DROPPED\nbetween the parser and TAST. Chain: `FunctionParam.meta` -\u003e\n`TypedParameter.ownership` -\u003e `HirParam.ownership` -\u003e\n`function_param_ownership` + `PARAM_OWNERSHIP` (qualified-name keyed) -\u003e\n`record_argument_moves`.\n\nUnannotated = `Owned`, so silence for code that says nothing is preserved.\n`@:owned` is documentation-only today (same as the default); `@:borrow` is the\nbehaviour change.\n\n**The name registry is NOT optional here.** For `@:move` a cross-context miss\nunder-reports; for `@:borrow` a miss makes correct code report use-after-move —\na FALSE POSITIVE. Any oracle case that is SILENT because of an annotation needs\na CONTROL that removes the annotation and flips it to ERROR, or the case passes\nfor the wrong reason.\n\nClosure capture is recorded as a **Read in the ENCLOSING function** at the point\nthe closure is built. Not a Move: `HirCaptureMode` is hardcoded to `ByValue` at\ntast_to_hir.rs:5664 and never computed, so it is not evidence of ownership\ntransfer.\n\n\nA receiver has no parameter to annotate, so the annotation goes on the method.\n`FunctionMetadata.memory_annotations` was ALREADY populated from a method's own\nmetadata (ast_lowering.rs:5788) and just stopped there — `MemoryAnnotation::Consume`\n+ `HirFunction.is_consume` is the whole wire. At the call site the receiver is no\nlonger skipped, so it holds slot 0 and the DECLARED PARAMETERS SHIFT BY ONE\n(`param_shift`); c17 pins that `@:borrow` still works on a consuming method.\n\nREJECTED: `this.__consume()` in the body. A call expression is legal where\nmetadata is not, but the CALLER needs the fact, and a statement can be\nconditional or unreachable — a declaration that participates in control flow is\nnot a declaration. (`this.__mutRef()` as an ACQUIRE is still a live idea — that\nis the RefCell shape, for dynamic exclusivity.)\n\n\n`lower_expression` did `lower_expression_inner(expr)?` with the recorders AFTER\nthe `?`. A void call has no destination register ⇒ `None` ⇒ early return ⇒ EVERY\nevent dropped. `take(a); take(a);` was SILENT with `:Void` and ERROR with `:Int`.\nSo `session.close();` — the idiomatic spelling of the feature — was unchecked.\n\n**THE ORACLE WAS GREEN THROUGH ALL OF IT: all 18 cases used an Int-returning\nhelper.** A helper's RETURN TYPE is part of a case. Found by an adversarial\nworkflow, not the suite.\n\n\nBefore it, `@:borrow` had NEGATIVE value: it silenced the check that works and\npromised nothing. `keep(@:borrow r:Res):Res { return r; }` printed 99.\nA borrowed param may not be returned, stored through a field/index, or captured.\nChecked AT THE ESCAPE SITE, no dataflow — an escape on any path is an escape.\n`borrow_roots` maps aliases to their root so `var t = r; return t;` fires and the\nmessage names the alias. Reading a field (`return r.v`) is NOT an escape — c23\npins it; a rule that rejected it would make `@:borrow` useless rather than safe.\n\n**LANDMINE FOUND DOING IT: `lower_function_body` (Pass 2) had NO param enrolment.**\nPass 1 registers the signature, Pass 2 lowers the body, and only `lower_function`\n/ `lower_instance_method` enrolled — so functions lowered there had NO tracked\nparameters and every check on them passed VACUOUSLY. The escape rule fired\nnowhere until this was fixed. Suspect vacuous passes whenever a SILENT case is\n\"working\".\n\n\n`docs/architecture/MEMORY_MANAGEMENT.md` now carries the ownership rules AND the\nRefMut plan. READ IT BEFORE RE-DESIGNING THIS.\n\n**WORKS TODAY, no compiler change:** acquiring is a move (ctor args always move), so\ntwo acquires / reading the owner while borrowed / mutating it through a method are all\nE0382. **The contract is NOT obvious: `@:move` on the OWNER arms the check; `@:move` on\nthe HANDLE is separately needed or handles duplicate by assignment; `@:safety` is\nINERT** (so `c5_legacy`'s README rationale was wrong — it is silent for lack of\n`@:move`, not lack of `@:safety`).\n\n**SIX PREREQUISITES, all measured, each ~the size of the rest of the model:**\n1. Release has NO HOOK — if/else arms, nested blocks, switch arms and function bodies\n   push NO drop scope; `exit_drop_scope` fires only at loop-body tails, in the merge\n   block after the real scope ended.\n2. `@:autoDeref`'s mandated `get():T` IS the exclusivity hole — hands back a second\n   unrestricted owner; private does not help, privacy is NOT ENFORCED.\n3. ~~Acquiring from a FIELD is silent~~ **CLOSED 4adaecbf: E0384 refuses moving a\n   `@:move` value OUT of a field** (owned arg / binding / assignment). Borrow, read and\n   method-receiver through a field are all still fine — c30/c31 are the controls, and a\n   rule that caught those would make `@:move` unusable as a field type.\n4. `@:shared` owners make it a total NO-OP — that is the Tensor family.\n5. **THE HANDLE LEAKS THE OWNER.** 16-byte malloc per acquire; SRA erases it UNLESS the\n   handle pointer reaches a non-inlined callee (i.e. the lending idiom). Then the ctor's\n   store makes `insert_free` stop freeing the OWNER: **66 MB over 4M acquires, linear,\n   no diagnostic.** MEASURED RULE: **lend `h.get()`, NEVER `h`** — that restores both.\n6. Disjoint field borrows (the headline use case) need a place domain.\n\n\nBoth workflows this session found real bugs the 184-suite and a green oracle missed:\n- **Void calls dropped every event** (1ce87835) — oracle was 18/18 with all-Int helpers.\n- **A ctor laundered a borrow past E0383** (653dbb7d) — `return new Wrap(r)`, silent.\n- **Moving out of a FIELD was unchecked** (4adaecbf) — `take(h.r); take(h.r);` silent.\n- **STILL OPEN: `@:autoDeref` disarms `@:consume`.** `h.close()` becomes\n  `h.get().close()`; a call-result receiver names no binding, so c20's shape flips from\n  ERROR to running the use-after-consume. Needs a place domain; documented, not fixed.\n\n**LESSON: ship, then attack. Adversarial passes found what the suite structurally could\nnot, twice.**\n\n\n- **`MarkMoved`/`CheckLive` is REDUNDANT, not a backstop — consider DELETING.**\n  Measured: `c4_reassign` emits 1 `CheckLive` and **0 `MarkMoved`**, so the\n  liveness slot is seeded live and the check CANNOT FAIL — a stack slot, load,\n  branch and panic block per read, for nothing. It is register-keyed (the\n  keying that made it reject correct code, f0e373b7) and fires at ONE syntactic\n  site. MoveFlow's event set is a superset of its trigger set and a may-analysis\n  fires wherever a runtime check would, so it is fully subsumed. `q3_marker`\n  also shows CheckLive emitted TWICE for one read (field/access.rs:1166).\n- **`Ref\u003cT\u003e` IS A LIE and has ZERO users outside the stdlib.** Its \"read-only\"\n  is enforced by NOTHING: `r.free()` on a `Ref\u003cInt\u003e` compiles and runs (Box's\n  method). `find_classes_with_method` (runtime_mapping.rs:776) filters on\n  `sig.method == method` ALONE. PARTIAL FIX 3f0ec161 constrains that scan by the\n  receiver's class — but `Ref.free()` is claimed by an EARLIER probe in the\n  21-probe chain and never reaches it, so the case is STILL OPEN.\n  `RAYZOR_DEBUG_RECV=1` prints what does reach the scan. Also: an unknown method\n  (`r.madeUp()`) produces NO DIAGNOSTIC and silently does not run.\n- **`RefMut\u003cT\u003e` as an extern abstract CANNOT WORK.**\n  `resolve_receiver_class_symbol` (resolve/classes.rs:25) matches Class /\n  TypeAlias / GenericInstance / `_ =\u003e break` — there is NO Abstract arm, so\n  `@:move` on an abstract is INERT. A borrow handle must be a CLASS.\n  And the `@:move`-class pattern gives TRANSFER, not borrowing: `@:move` must be\n  on the OWNER type (not the handle), and the owner never comes back — MoveFlow\n  revives only on reassignment, never at scope exit.\n- Constructor params carry no ownership, so `new Foo(a)` always moves.\n- `return a` is not recorded as a move (harmless: nothing follows in-function).\n- No `@:move` exists anywhere in nue or the stdlib, so blast radius today is\n  zero by construction — real usage has not stress-tested this.\n\nRelated: [[bugs_ownership_analysis_never_runs]],\n[[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]], [[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_moveflow_mir_move_checker` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}